[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186316563 :answer-field/choices 17592186315862] {:db/id 17592186217802 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186316563 :answer-field/correct 17592186316274 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b516dd7-0e0d-5e63-914a-7e40f288e42a" "question-0445e2f8-6c9c-5e30-8a27-7e22ee9c426c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b516dd7-0e0d-5e63-914a-7e40f288e42a" :question/id #uuid "4b516dd7-0e0d-5e63-914a-7e40f288e42a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343270" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(-1)=6.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(-1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(-1) & =C{e}^{-(-1)} \\\\ 6 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =6{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-2}=6{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-beb68067-4a29-5062-a362-e53691a64280"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-beb68067-4a29-5062-a362-e53691a64280" :answer-field/id #uuid "beb68067-4a29-5062-a362-e53691a64280" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0c115289-a055-5b27-80d7-61934239ac5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c115289-a055-5b27-80d7-61934239ac5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c115289-a055-5b27-80d7-61934239ac5f" :answer/id #uuid "0c115289-a055-5b27-80d7-61934239ac5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{e}^{-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0445e2f8-6c9c-5e30-8a27-7e22ee9c426c" :question/id #uuid "0445e2f8-6c9c-5e30-8a27-7e22ee9c426c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343282" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+1)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=6.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{12}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{12}{3+1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5aac8d3-3126-59bd-b7a3-08af8b0916d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5aac8d3-3126-59bd-b7a3-08af8b0916d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5aac8d3-3126-59bd-b7a3-08af8b0916d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a6c623a5-d649-5a54-8c2b-52ae41579420" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6c623a5-d649-5a54-8c2b-52ae41579420"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6c623a5-d649-5a54-8c2b-52ae41579420" :answer/id #uuid "a6c623a5-d649-5a54-8c2b-52ae41579420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186397617 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=-1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=-1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}